      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate markdown carefully.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Certificate No: R76/1992-GB1-10.04\n- Revision 3\n- Issuing authority: National Measurement Office\n- Member State: United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\n- Issue Date: 19 May 2014\n- Reference No: T1128/0107 (this might be the project number or test report reference)\n- Scheme: This is an OIML MAA certificate? It says \"OIML MUTUAL ACCEPTANCE ARRANGEMENT\" — the MAA scheme. Actually the certificate number format \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\" — the scheme would be under R76. The MAA mention... The scheme field is (A|B). Hmm. MAA certificates... Actually OIML-CS has Scheme A and Scheme B. MAA certificates predate the OIML-CS. The certificate header mentions \"MUTUAL ACCEPTANCE ARRANGEMENT\". For simplicity, I could leave scheme out or infer. The number \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\" doesn't include a letter prefix indicating scheme A/B (like \"R60/2000-GB1-10.04\" is typical for basic OIML certs). MAA certificates are not basic OIML certificates. I'll omit scheme since not clearly stated. Actually the schema says scheme (A|B). If not determinable, omit per rule 8 (don't fabricate).\n\n- page_total: \"This certificate includes 4 pages\" → 4\n- project_number: \"Reference No: T1128/0107\" — this could be the project number. The test report is \"pattern evaluation report P00945\". Project number could be T1128/0107. I'll put project_number: \"T1128/0107\".\n- oiml_issuer_id: UKAS PRODUCT CERTIFICATION 0135 — hmm, the \"0135\" might be a UKAS certification number. There's no explicit OIML issuer ID. Omit.\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- name: National Measurement Office\n- address_lines: [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n- person_responsible: Paul Dixon\n- person_title: Director, Product Certification\n- phone: +44 (0)20 8943 7272\n- fax: not in schema, skip\n- website: www.gov.uk/nmo\n- Signatory: G Stones\n\n**Applicants:**\n- CAS Corporation, #262, Geurugogae-ro, Gwangjeok-myeon, Yangju-si, Gyeonggi-do, Republic of Korea\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- \"The applicant\" → CAS Corporation\n- Plus: Shanghai CAS Electronics Co., Ltd (Maixinroad 448, Xinqiaozhen, Songjiangqu, Shanghai, China)\n- CAS Elektronik San. Tic. A.S. (Yukari Dudulu, Bostancı Cad. Mevdudi Sokak No: 34, Umraniye-Istanbul, Turkey)\n- Alternative manufacturer: CAS (Zhejiang) Electronics Co., Ltd (99# Changjiang Road, Jiashan County, Zhejiang Province, China)\n\nSo manufacturers[] should include CAS Corporation (the applicant, as manufacturer of certified pattern) plus the three named companies.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- category: \"Weighing instrument\" — schema says examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", ... I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" or \"Weighing instrument\". R76 is for non-automatic weighing instruments. I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\".\n- type_designations: [\"SW Series\"] plus models ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS-M, SW-1, ER JR, PW-II and variants SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR.\n- description: \"Class III, mains or battery-operated, self-indicating, non-automatic weighing instruments designated SW Series...\"\n\n**Model family:**\nThis is a big family. Models: ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS-M (ER PLUS / ER PLUS M column), SW-1 variants (SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR), ER JR, PW-II. And each comes in single-interval and dual-interval variants with multiple Max capacities.\n\nThis is complex. The tables are essentially matrix tables: Model × capacity variants, with Max, Min, e rows.\n\nHow to structure model_level characteristics? Each model has multiple capacity variants. I could define model_ids like \"ED (single-interval, Max=3 kg)\" etc. Or use matrix_tables to preserve the tables and provide model_level entries for max/min/e per variant.\n\nLet me enumerate the variants:\n\nSingle-interval:\n- ED: Max 3, 6, 15, 30 kg; Min 20, 40, 100, 200 g; e 1, 2, 5, 10 g\n- ER PLUS / ER PLUS M: Max 6, 15, 30; Min 40, 100, 200; e 2, 5, 10\n- SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR: Max 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15, 20, 30; Min 20, 20, 40, 40, 100, 100, 200, 200; e 1, 1, 2, 2, 5, 5, 10, 10\n- ER JR: Max 6, 15, 30; Min 40, 100, 200; e 2, 5, 10\n- PW-II: Max 2, 5, 10; Min 20, 40, 100; e 1, 2, 5\n\nDual-interval:\n- ED: Max 1.5/3, 3/6, 6/15, 15/30; Min 10, 20, 40, 100; e 0.5/1, 1/2, 2/5, 5/10\n- ER PLUS / ER PLUS M: Max 3/6, 6/15, 15/30; Min 20, 40, 100; e 1/2, 2/5, 5/10\n- SW-1*: Max 1/2, 1.5/3, 2.5/5, 3/6, 4/10, 6/15, 10/20, 15/30; Min 10, 10, 20, 20, 40, 40, 100, 100; e 0.5/1, 0.5/1, 1/2, 1/2, 2/5, 2/5, 5/10, 5/10\n- ER JR: Max 3/6, 6/15, 15/30; Min 20, 40, 100; e 1/2, 2/5, 5/10\n- PW-II: Max 1/2, 2.5/5, 4/10; Min 10, 20, 40; e 0.5/1, 1/2, 2/5\n\nThe best representation: matrix_tables for each table (6 tables), plus model_family.models with model_id like \"ED single-interval 3 kg\" etc. And model_level characteristics for max, min, e per variant.\n\nGiven the complexity, I think the cleanest approach:\n\n1. `matrix_tables`: 6 entries preserving the tables as-is.\n2. `model_family.models`: enumerate each variant with attributes: model, interval_type (single/dual), max_kg (or range), min_g, e_g.\n\nActually the model_id should be unique per variant. E.g., \"ED single-interval Max=3kg\", \"ED dual-interval Max=1.5/3kg\". That's a lot of model entries: 4+3+8+3+3 = 21 single-interval + 4+3+8+3+3 = 21 dual = 42 variants. That's a lot but it's faithful.\n\nAlternatively, treat \"model\" axis as the model name (ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1S, ..., ER JR, PW-II) and put Max/Min/e in config_level with axis \"capacity variant\". Hmm.\n\nThe schema says model_level[] is \"attribute × model matrix\" with values[].config_level is for configuration axes.\n\nGiven the certificate covers variants by capacity, I think it's most reasonable to:\n- model_family.models: one entry per model variant combining model name + interval type + capacity. This gives 42 entries. That's heavy but accurate.\n\nAlternatively, keep models as the named models (ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II) — 10 models — and represent the capacity variants via config_level with axis \"capacity variant\" per interval type.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's most useful. The R60 example in the rules creates model_id like \"Emax=25t\" for each capacity variant. Following that precedent, I should create model_ids per capacity variant.\n\nLet me define model_id format: \"ED single-interval 3 kg\" or \"ED-3kg (single-interval)\". Maybe simpler: \"ED SI 3 kg\". Let me use something descriptive: \"ED single-interval Max 3 kg\". And dual: \"ED dual-interval Max 1.5/3 kg\".\n\nFor dual-interval, max is a pair (Max1/Max2), e is a pair (e1/e2). Value could be represented as a string \"1.5 / 3\" since it's two values. Hmm, rules say numbers not strings, but dual values are genuinely two values. For dual-interval instruments, Max is given as \"1.5 / 3\" meaning Max1/Max2. I could represent as string, or as {min: 1.5, max: 3}? No — that would be wrong semantically; it's not a range, it's two intervals. I'll keep as string \"1.5/3\" with a note, or maybe use value as object? The schema says value can be number | {min,max} | string. For dual-interval, string seems most honest: \"1.5 / 3\".\n\nActually, let me reconsider. For dual-interval instruments:\n- Max: 1.5/3 → Max₁ = 1.5 kg, Max₂ = 3 kg\n- e: 0.5/1 → e₁ = 0.5 g, e₂ = 1 g\n\nI could split into separate attributes: maximum_capacity_max1, maximum_capacity_max2... that's overkill. I'll keep as strings \"1.5 / 3\" for dual-interval in the matrix tables, and for model_level maybe use string values.\n\nGiven the scale, maybe the cleanest is:\n\n- matrix_tables: 6 tables preserving everything faithfully with columns.\n- model_family.models: entries per model (with model_id like \"ED\", and attributes listing variants?). Hmm.\n\nLet me look at how the R137 example handles it: models[] with one model_id per size, and model_level attributes with values per model. So they flatten.\n\nFor this cert, flattening to 42 variants:\n\nSingle-interval:\n1. ED SI 3kg: Max 3, Min 20g, e 1g\n2. ED SI 6kg: Max 6, Min 40g, e 2g\n3. ED SI 15kg: Max 15, Min 100g, e 5g\n4. ED SI 30kg: Max 30, Min 200g, e 10g\n5. ER PLUS/ER PLUS M SI 6kg: Max 6, Min 40, e 2\n6. ER PLUS/ER PLUS M SI 15kg: Max 15, Min 100, e 5\n7. ER PLUS/ER PLUS M SI 30kg: Max 30, Min 200, e 10\n8. SW-1* SI 2kg: Max 2, Min 20, e 1\n9. SW-1* SI 3kg: Max 3, Min 20, e 1\n10. SW-1* SI 5kg: Max 5, Min 40, e 2\n11. SW-1* SI 6kg: Max 6, Min 40, e 2\n12. SW-1* SI 10kg: Max 10, Min 100, e 5\n13. SW-1* SI 15kg: Max 15, Min 100, e 5\n14. SW-1* SI 20kg: Max 20, Min 200, e 10\n15. SW-1* SI 30kg: Max 30, Min 200, e 10\n16. ER JR SI 6kg: Max 6, Min 40, e 2\n17. ER JR SI 15kg: Max 15, Min 100, e 5\n18. ER JR SI 30kg: Max 30, Min 200, e 10\n19. PW-II SI 2kg: Max 2, Min 20, e 1\n20. PW-II SI 5kg: Max 5, Min 40, e 2\n21. PW-II SI 10kg: Max 10, Min 100, e 5\n\nDual-interval:\n22. ED DI 1.5/3: Max \"1.5/3\", Min 10, e \"0.5/1\"\n23. ED DI 3/6: Max \"3/6\", Min 20, e \"1/2\"\n24. ED DI 6/15: \"6/15\", 40, \"2/5\"\n25. ED DI 15/30: \"15/30\", 100, \"5/10\"\n26. ER PLUS DI 3/6: \"3/6\", 20, \"1/2\"\n27. ER PLUS DI 6/15: \"6/15\", 40, \"2/5\"\n28. ER PLUS DI 15/30: \"15/30\", 100, \"5/10\"\n29. SW-1 DI 1/2: \"1/2\", 10, \"0.5/1\"\n30. SW-1 DI 1.5/3: \"1.5/3\", 10, \"0.5/1\"\n31. SW-1 DI 2.5/5: \"2.5/5\", 20, \"1/2\"\n32. SW-1 DI 3/6: \"3/6\", 20, \"1/2\"\n33. SW-1 DI 4/10: \"4/10\", 40, \"2/5\"\n34. SW-1 DI 6/15: \"6/15\", 40, \"2/5\"\n35. SW-1 DI 10/20: \"10/20\", 100, \"5/10\"\n36. SW-1 DI 15/30: \"15/30\", 100, \"5/10\"\n37. ER JR DI 3/6: \"3/6\", 20, \"1/2\"\n38. ER JR DI 6/15: \"6/15\", 40, \"2/5\"\n39. ER JR DI 15/30: \"15/30\", 100, \"5/10\"\n40. PW-II DI 1/2: \"1/2\", 10, \"0.5/1\"\n41. PW-II DI 2.5/5: \"2.5/5\", 20, \"1/2\"\n42. PW-II DI 4/10: \"4/10\", 40, \"2/5\"\n\n42 models with 3 model_level attributes each = 126 value entries. That's large but doable. Actually, this might be overkill. Let me think about a balanced approach.\n\nAlternative: model_family.models lists the 10 named models (ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II), and model_level entries attribute × model... but attributes vary per capacity variant, not just per model. The capacity variants are essentially sub-variants.\n\nI think the 42-variant flattening is the most faithful and consistent with the R60 example (model_id \"Emax=25t\"). I'll use model_id format like \"ED single-interval Max 3 kg\" — maybe shorter: \"ED SI 3 kg\"? For clarity, \"ED (single-interval, Max 3 kg)\". Hmm, let me use: \"ED-SI-3kg\", \"ED-DI-1.5/3kg\". Or \"ED single 3 kg\".\n\nLet me go with: model_id: \"ED single-interval 3 kg\" and \"ED dual-interval 1.5/3 kg\". For the SW-1 family models sharing the same table (SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR), the model_id could be \"SW-1S/SW-1C/SW-1W/SW-1LR/SW-1WR single-interval 2 kg\" — too long. Maybe \"SW-1 series single-interval 2 kg\". The cert says \"SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR\" share the table. I'll use \"SW-1 (S/C/W/LR/WR) single-interval 2 kg\"? Hmm, or just label \"SW-1S/SW-1C/SW-1W/SW-1LR/SW-1WR SI 2 kg\".\n\nSimilarly \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\" share columns. I'll use \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single-interval 6 kg\".\n\nLet me decide the model_id naming: `{model} {interval}-interval {max} kg`, e.g., \"ED single-interval 3 kg\", \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single-interval 6 kg\", \"SW-1S/SW-1C/SW-1W/SW-1LR/SW-1WR dual-interval 1/2 kg\", \"PW-II dual-interval 1/2 kg\".\n\nAnd models[] entries include attributes: {\"interval_type\": \"single\", \"maximum_capacity\": {\"value\": 3, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\"}, ...}? The schema says attributes{} — I can put simple attributes. But to keep JSON smaller, maybe attributes with max/min/e as structured values. Hmm, the schema shows `attributes{}` as free-form. I could put the structured values there, and also populate model_level. That duplicates. To reduce duplication, I'll put attributes in models[] with simple values and also populate model_level matrices. Actually duplication is fine and expected per the R137 example (matrix_tables AND model_level both).\n\nTo keep it manageable, I'll do:\n- models[]: 42 entries, each with attributes: {\"interval_type\": \"single\"|\"dual\", \"maximum_capacity_kg\": 3, \"minimum_load_g\": 20, \"verification_scale_interval_g\": 1} — simple values.\n- model_level[]: 3 entries (maximum_capacity, minimum_load, verification_scale_interval e), values[] with 42 entries each using the same model_ids.\n\nActually wait — should the min and e be separate model_level entries? Yes: attributes: maximum_capacity (kg), minimum_capacity (g), scale_interval_e (g).\n\nHmm, but for dual-interval the values are strings \"1.5/3\". That's fine.\n\nAlso matrix_tables: 6 tables. Let me structure them:\n\nTable 1 (single-interval, ED & ER PLUS/ER PLUS M):\ncolumns: [\"model\", \"variant\", \"maximum_capacity_kg\", \"minimum_load_g\", \"verification_scale_interval_g\"]\nrows:\n- {model: \"ED\", variant: 1, maximum_capacity_kg: 3, minimum_load_g: 20, verification_scale_interval_g: 1}\n- {model: \"ED\", variant: 2, ...6, 40, 2}\n- {model: \"ED\", variant: 3, ...15, 100, 5}\n- {model: \"ED\", variant: 4, ...30, 200, 10}\n- {model: \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\", variant: 1, ...6, 40, 2}\n- {model: \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\", variant: 2, ...15, 100, 5}\n- {model: \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\", variant: 3, ...30, 200, 10}\n\nHmm, \"variant\" index is fabricated. Maybe skip variant column and just have rows with model repeated. That's fine — repeated model rows represent capacity variants.\n\nActually the schema for matrix_tables rows: \"rows[] (object per row)\". Free-form. I'll do: {\"model\": \"ED\", \"maximum_capacity_kg\": 3, \"minimum_load_g\": 20, \"verification_scale_interval_g\": 1}.\n\nTable names:\n1. \"Single-interval instruments — ED and ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\"\n2. \"Single-interval instruments — SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR\"\n3. \"Single-interval instruments — ER JR and PW-II\"\n4. \"Dual-interval instruments — ED and ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\"\n5. \"Dual-interval instruments — SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR\"\n6. \"Dual-interval instruments — ER JR and PW-II\"\n\nColumns: [\"model\", \"max_kg\", \"min_g\", \"e_g\"]. With column_units [null, \"kg\", \"g\", \"g\"]. Actually schema example includes column_units. I'll include them.\n\nFor dual-interval, values like \"1.5 / 3\" remain strings.\n\n**Components:**\n- Load cell: CAS load cell, model SW → components entry: role \"load cell\", type_designations [\"SW\"], manufacturer CAS.\nNo other components (the instrument is self-contained). Also there's an alternative manufacturer of the load cell? \"Alternative manufacturer: CAS (Zhejiang) Electronics Co., Ltd\" — this seems to be an alternative manufacturer of the certified instrument (or of the load cell?). Reading page 3: it's under its own heading \"Alternative manufacturer:\" — this is likely an alternative manufacturer of the whole instrument, similar to page 2 \"The manufacturer of the certified pattern is the Applicant, or the following companies: Shanghai CAS..., CAS Elektronik...\". The Zhejiang one was added in rev 3 as alternative manufacturer. So it belongs in manufacturers[].\n\n**Characteristics type_level:**\n- accuracy_class: \"III\" → value \"III\"? Accuracy class III. Represent as {value: \"III\", unit_symbol: null}. Hmm, value must be number|minmax|string — \"III\" string OK.\n- temperature_range: -10 to +40 °C → {value: {min: -10, max: 40}, unit_symbol: \"°C\", unit_id: \"u:degree_Celsius\"}\n- power supply: 12 V DC (ED, ER PLUS, SW-1 and ER JR Models) or 9 V DC (PW-II Model). This is model-dependent → model_level? The models here are grouped: 12V applies to ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS-M?, SW-1, ER JR; 9V to PW-II. Hmm, model_level entries with values per model-group. Could do:\n  - supply_voltage model_level: values: [{model: \"ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1 series, ER JR\", value: 12}, {model: \"PW-II\", value: 9}], unit_symbol \"V\". Plus type_level power_supply_type: \"DC\".\n  \n  Or config_level. I think model_level is appropriate since it varies by model.\n\n  Alternatively keep in description. But rules prefer structured. I'll add model_level entry \"power_supply_voltage\" with grouped models. For the model field in values, I'll use group strings.\n\n- Batteries: varies by model: \n  - integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah battery (ED, ER PLUS and ER JR Models)\n  - integrated 4 x 1.5 V D type batteries (ER JR Model)\n  - integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.5 Ah battery (SW-1 Model)\n  - integrated 6 x 1.5 V AA batteries (PW-II Model)\n  This is messy; could be a config_level or just description. I'll capture as a matrix_table? Or config_level with axis \"model\"? Hmm. Maybe simpler: put battery options in type_level as a list? Not allowed. I could add to description text. The certified_type.description includes the main description; I can extend the description or create components. Let me add config_level entries:\n  - attribute: \"battery_option\", axis: \"model\", values: [{condition: \"ED, ER PLUS, ER JR\", value: \"rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah\"}, ...]. Values as strings.\n  \n  Actually config_level schema: values[]: {condition, value}. value should follow structured form {value, unit_symbol...}? Rule 5 says \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". Hmm ambiguous. In example schema: config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}. I'll make each entry: {condition: \"...\", value: {...}, unit_symbol: ...} — hmm. Let me just do {condition, value, unit_symbol} where value is number or string.\n\n  Let me add:\n  - attribute \"initial_zero_setting_range\", axis \"Max\": value {min:null, max:20}, unit \"%\"? \"≤ 20% of Max\" → value 20, unit \"%\", reference Max. I'll do attribute initial_zero_setting_device_range, unit \"% of Max\" or \"%\" with a note. Keep unit_symbol \"%\", value {min:null,max:20}? Hmm \"≤ 20%\" → max 20. I'll do value: 20, unit_symbol: \"%\", maybe condition/reference. Simpler: value {min:null, max:20}? Rules: ≤2.5 → {min:null, max:2.5}. So ≤20 → {min:null, max:20}. Unit \"%\" — though technically \"of Max\". I'll put attribute \"initial_zero_setting_range\" and add unit_symbol \"%\" and maybe a separate note in description. Fine.\n  - semi-automatic zero setting range: ≤ 4% of Max → similar.\n  - zero tracking range: ≤ 4% of Max.\n  - tare: T ≤ -50% Max (single-interval); T ≤ -Max₁ (dual-interval). Represent: attribute \"maximum_subtractive_tare\", config_level axis \"interval_type\": values: [{condition: \"single-interval\", value: -50, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"dual-interval\", value: \"-Max1\", unit_symbol: null}]. Hmm value -50 with negative sign: \"T ≤ -50% Max\" means subtractive tare up to 50% of Max. Value could be -50 (as stated) or 50. I'll keep -50 to be faithful? T ≤ -50% Max... The tare setting T is ≤ -50% Max, i.e., tare subtracts up to 50% Max. I'll record value -50, unit \"%\". Or value \"50% of Max\". Hmm. Let me record as value: {min: null, max: -50}? That's weird. I'll just use value: -50, unit_symbol: \"% of Max\". Actually unit_symbol should be a unit; \"% of Max\" is not a unit but acceptable as symbol? For semi-auto zero it's \"% of Max\" too. Let me use unit_symbol \"%\" and encode the reference in attribute name: \"initial_zero_setting_range_percent_of_max\"? Hmm clunky. \n\n  Alternative: keep unit_symbol null and value as string \"≤ 20% of Max\"? But rule 2 says numbers not strings. But also \"≤ 20% of Max\" isn't a plain number. I think best: attribute \"initial_zero_setting_range\", value {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol \"%\". The \"of Max\" reference: add to attribute label? The canonical label examples include \"ratio_y_emax_over_vmin\" encoding the reference. So maybe \"initial_zero_setting_range_percent_of_max\". Hmm, but then unit % and reference max encoded in name. I'll do attribute names: \"initial_zero_setting_range\" with unit \"%\" — and describe reference in certified_type.description? The description already covers instruments generally. \n\n  I'll keep it simple: attribute names as canonical, unit \"%\", and I'll note \"(of Max)\" is implicit. Actually, to be safer, I'll name them: initial_zero_setting_range_of_max, semi_automatic_zero_setting_range_of_max, zero_tracking_range_of_max, subtractive_tare_range_of_max. That encodes reference. Unit \"%\". Good.\n\n**Devices list** — these are features, could go into description or components. I'll summarize in description and capture key numeric ones as characteristics.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: \"R76\" (canonical), edition: 1992, amendment: null (1992(E) — the (E) is the English language edition marker, not amendment). scheme: not stated (MAA certificate). accuracy_classes: [\"III\"].\n\n**Test reports:**\n- pattern evaluation report P00945, 24 pages, role: type_evaluation_report. date not given. id \"P00945\".\n- Also \"Reference No: T1128/0107\" — that's the NMO project/application reference. Put in project_number.\n\n**Revision history:**\n- \"\" → 27 May 2009, \"Certificate first issued\"\n- \"1\" → 04 September 2012, \"Load cell and technical data sections added.\"\n- \"2\" → 12 May 2014, \"Single-interval instruments added. Max tare value added to devices section. Sealing section added.\"\n- \"3\" → 19 May 2014, \"Applicant's address changed from: 19 Ganap-Ri, Gwangjuk-Myoun, Yangju-Si, Gyeonggi-Do 482-841, Republic of Korea. Alternative manufacturer added.\"\n\n**Footnotes:** none. Omit.\n\n**Scheme:** The header says \"OIML MUTUAL ACCEPTANCE ARRANGEMENT\" — MAA certificates are separate from OIML-CS basic certificates. The schema asks scheme (A|B). Not clearly stated → omit. Also \"OIML R76 MAA Rev. 1 01 April 2011\" footer refers to the MAA certificate template revision, not the certificate revision. Skip.\n\n**member_state:** \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\" — I'll put \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\".\n\n**date_issued:** \"2014-05-19\" — format? Probably ISO. The schema doesn't specify; I'll use \"2014-05-19\".\n\n**certificate.number:** \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\". Also revision 3 — there's no \"revision\" field at cert level in schema; revision_history covers it. Maybe include revision info in revision_history only.\n\n**page_total:** 4.\n\nNow the models. Let me carefully build the model list and the three model_level matrices.\n\nModel groups:\n- ED (single & dual)\n- ER PLUS / ER PLUS M (single & dual) — note text says \"ER PLUS-M\" but table says \"ER PLUS M\"; type_designations should include \"ER PLUS-M\" and \"ER PLUS M\"? I'll use \"ER PLUS M\" from table and \"ER PLUS-M\" from description... Use \"ER PLUS-M\" as in description and note table. I'll include both variants in type_designations? To avoid clutter, type_designations: [\"SW Series\", \"ED\", \"ER PLUS\", \"ER PLUS-M\", \"SW-1\", \"SW-1S\", \"SW-1C\", \"SW-1W\", \"SW-1LR\", \"SW-1WR\", \"ER JR\", \"PW-II\"].\n\nModel IDs for the family (using table group labels):\n- \"ED single-interval Max 3 kg\"\n- \"ED single-interval Max 6 kg\"\n- \"ED single-interval Max 15 kg\"\n- \"ED single-interval Max 30 kg\"\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single-interval Max 6 kg\"\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single-interval Max 15 kg\"\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single-interval Max 30 kg\"\n- \"SW-1S/SW-1C/SW-1W/SW-1LR/SW-1WR single-interval Max 2 kg\"\n- \"... single-interval Max 3 kg\"\n- \"... 5 kg\"\n- \"... 6 kg\"\n- \"... 10 kg\"\n- \"... 15 kg\"\n- \"... 20 kg\"\n- \"... 30 kg\"\n- \"ER JR single-interval Max 6 kg\"\n- \"ER JR single-interval Max 15 kg\"\n- \"ER JR single-interval Max 30 kg\"\n- \"PW-II single-interval Max 2 kg\"\n- \"PW-II single-interval Max 5 kg\"\n- \"PW-II single-interval Max 10 kg\"\n- Dual:\n- \"ED dual-interval Max 1.5/3 kg\"\n- \"ED dual-interval Max 3/6 kg\"\n- \"ED dual-interval Max 6/15 kg\"\n- \"ED dual-interval Max 15/30 kg\"\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M dual-interval Max 3/6 kg\"\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M dual-interval Max 6/15 kg\"\n- \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M dual-interval Max 15/30 kg\"\n- \"SW-1S/SW-1C/SW-1W/SW-1LR/SW-1WR dual-interval Max 1/2 kg\"\n- \"... 1.5/3 kg\"\n- \"... 2.5/5 kg\"\n- \"... 3/6 kg\"\n- \"... 4/10 kg\"\n- \"... 6/15 kg\"\n- \"... 10/20 kg\"\n- \"... 15/30 kg\"\n- \"ER JR dual-interval Max 3/6 kg\"\n- \"ER JR dual-interval Max 6/15 kg\"\n- \"ER JR dual-interval Max 15/30 kg\"\n- \"PW-II dual-interval Max 1/2 kg\"\n- \"PW-II dual-interval Max 2.5/5 kg\"\n- \"PW-II dual-interval Max 4/10 kg\"\n\nTotal 42 models. For models[] entries, include attributes: interval_type, maximum_capacity (structured), minimum_load (structured), scale_interval (structured). That's a lot of JSON but fine.\n\nHmm, maybe I should simplify the model_id to shorter forms: \"ED SI 3 kg\". But full words are clearer. I'll use a medium form: \"ED single-interval 3 kg\", \"ER PLUS/ER PLUS M dual-interval 3/6 kg\", \"SW-1S/... dual-interval 1/2 kg\" — no, spell out.\n\nActually for the SW-1 group, model_id could be \"SW-1 (S/C/W/LR/WR) dual-interval 1/2 kg\"? The cert writes \"SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR\". I'll write \"SW-1S/SW-1C/SW-1W/SW-1LR/SW-1WR\". Long but faithful.\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n1. attribute \"maximum_capacity\", unit_symbol \"kg\", values: 42 entries. Single: numbers; dual: strings \"1.5 / 3\".\n2. attribute \"minimum_capacity\", unit_symbol \"g\", values: 42 entries.\n3. attribute \"verification_scale_interval\" (e), unit_symbol \"g\", values: 42 entries. Dual: strings \"0.5 / 1\".\n\nAnd power supply voltage model_level:\n- attribute \"power_supply_voltage\", unit_symbol \"V\": values: [{model: \"ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1 (S/C/W/LR/WR), ER JR\", value: 12}, {model: \"PW-II\", value: 9}]. And power_supply_type \"DC\" at type_level.\n\nHmm, but the models in values should ideally match model_ids. Using group labels is a pragmatic compromise. Alternatively list per-model: 12 V for ED..., 9 V for PW-II — many entries. I'll use group strings: \"ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1S..SW-1WR, ER JR\". Let me write {model: \"ED / ER PLUS / ER PLUS M / SW-1 series / ER JR\", value: 12} and {model: \"PW-II\", value: 9}.\n\nBattery options via config_level:\n- attribute \"battery_option\", axis \"model\", values:\n  - {condition: \"ED, ER PLUS, ER JR\", value: \"integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah\"}\n  - {condition: \"ER JR\", value: \"integrated 4 x 1.5 V D type\"}\n  - {condition: \"SW-1\", value: \"integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.5 Ah\"}\n  - {condition: \"PW-II\", value: \"integrated 6 x 1.5 V AA\"}\nValues as strings (battery descriptions aren't simple numbers). unit_symbol null.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"III\"}\n- temperature_range: {value: {min: -10, max: 40}, unit_symbol: \"°C\", unit_id: \"u:degree_Celsius\"}\n- initial_zero_setting_range_of_max: {value: {min:null, max:20}, unit_symbol: \"%\"} — hmm, rule: ≤2.5m/s → {min:null,max:2.5}. OK.\n- semi_automatic_zero_setting_range_of_max: {value: {min:null,max:4}, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n- zero_tracking_range_of_max: {value: {min:null,max:4}, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"DC\"}\n- construction features: steel construction, keypad, stainless load receptor, level indicator, operator & customer display — description.\n\nTare config_level:\n- attribute \"subtractive_tare_range\", axis \"interval type\", values:\n  - {condition: \"single-interval\", value: -50, unit_symbol: \"% of Max\"}? Hmm. \"T ≤ -50 % Max\" — the value is -50% of Max. I'll write {condition: \"single-interval instruments\", value: -50, unit_symbol: \"%\"} and note. Or value string \"≤ -50 % Max\". Rules prefer structured. value: -50, unit_symbol: \"%\". OK.\n  - {condition: \"dual-interval instruments\", value: \"-Max1\", unit_symbol: null} — formula-like, keep as string.\n\nHmm wait, rule says pure formulas keep as string: \"T ≤ -Max₁\" → value \"-Max1\" string is fine.\n\nAlso \"gravity compensation\", \"zero indicator\", \"net indicator\", \"check weighing mode\" — description.\n\nComponents:\n- {role: \"load cell\", type_designations: [\"SW\"], characteristics: {manufacturer: \"CAS\"}}? characteristics{} free-form — I'll put {\"manufacturer\": \"CAS\", \"description\": \"CAS load cell, model SW\"}. Keep simple: role \"load cell\", type_designations [\"SW\"], characteristics {\"manufacturer\": \"CAS\"}.\n\nApplicants: one entry: CAS Corporation with address lines [\"#262, Geurugogae-ro\", \"Gwangjeok-myeon\", \"Yangju-si\", \"Gyeonggi-do\", \"Republic of Korea\"].\n\nManufacturers: \n1. CAS Corporation (same address) — \"The applicant\" is the manufacturer.\n2. Shanghai CAS Electronics Co., Ltd — [\"Maixinroad 448\", \"Xinqiaozhen\", \"Songjiangqu\", \"Shanghai\", \"China\"]\n3. CAS Elektronik San. Tic. A.S. — [\"Yukari Dudulu, Bostancı Cad. Mevdudi Sokak No: 34\", \"Umraniye-Istanbul\", \"Turkey\"] (the OCR shows \"Umtraniye\" typo; I'll keep \"Umraniye\" as close to source — actually source says \"Umtraniye-Istanbul\". I'll keep as written: \"Umtraniye-Istanbul / Turkey\" — hmm, better split: address_lines: [\"Yukari Dudulu, Bostancı Cad. Mevdudi Sokak No: 34\", \"Umtraniye-Istanbul\", \"Turkey\"].\n4. CAS (Zhejiang) Electronics Co., Ltd — [\"99# Changjiang Road\", \"Jiashan County\", \"Zhejiang Province\", \"China\"] (role: alternative manufacturer — no role field; maybe add \"role\": \"alternative manufacturer\"? Schema has only name, address_lines. Rule 8: don't fabricate fields. I could add a note... I'll just list them; maybe add an extra key \"role\" — schema doesn't forbid extra keys? Safer to stick to schema. But it might be valuable. The schema strictly: manufacturers[]: name, address_lines[]. I'll stick to that.\n\nTest reports: [{id: \"P00945\", pages: 24, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}]. Date not given.\n\nIssuing authority details:\n- name: \"National Measurement Office\"\n- address_lines: [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Paul Dixon\"\n- person_title: \"Director, Product Certification\"\n- phone: \"+44 (0)20 8943 7272\"\n- website: \"www.gov.uk/nmo\"\n\nAlso signatory G Stones — not in schema; skip or add to... there's no field. Skip.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\"\n- scheme: omit (MAA, not A/B)\n- project_number: \"T1128/0107\"\n- page_total: 4\n- member_state: \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\"\n- date_issued: \"2014-05-19\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: omit.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\"\n- type_designations: [\"SW Series\", \"ED\", \"ER PLUS\", \"ER PLUS-M\", \"SW-1\", \"SW-1S\", \"SW-1C\", \"SW-1W\", \"SW-1LR\", \"SW-1WR\", \"ER JR\", \"PW-II\"]\n- module_designation: null\n- description: \"Class III, mains or battery-operated, self-indicating, non-automatic weighing instruments designated SW Series and comprising the ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS-M, SW-1, ER JR and PW-II models and their variants. Steel construction with operator's keypad, stainless steel load receptor, level indicator, operator and customer display. Devices: initial zero setting device, semi-automatic zero setting device, zero tracking device, zero indicator, net indicator, semi-automatic subtractive tare balancing device, gravity compensation, check weighing and weighing unstable samples modes (SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR, PW-II and ED models only). Load cell: CAS load cell model SW. Sealing: wire-and-seal or tamper-evident sticker preventing access to load cell and calibration switch.\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"SW Series\"\n- models: 42 entries.\n\nThat's long but let's write it.\n\nWait — should I really produce 42 models? Consider size: each model entry ~1 line in JSON minified. 42 entries × 3 matrices = manageable. Let me be consistent.\n\nLet me set canonical model IDs (short but clear):\n\nSingle-interval:\nS1: \"ED SI Max 3 kg\"? I'd rather avoid abbreviations. Use \"ED single-interval Max 3 kg\". OK let me just carefully write.\n\nActually, to reduce verbosity, model_id format: \"{Model} {single|dual}-interval {Max} kg\". e.g. \"ED single-interval 3 kg\". The Max/Min/e go into attributes and model_level anyway. Drop \"Max\" word: \"ED single-interval 3 kg\" is clear enough. Dual: \"ED dual-interval 1.5/3 kg\".\n\nmodels[] attributes: I'll include: {\"interval_type\": \"single\", \"maximum_capacity\": 3, \"minimum_capacity\": 20, \"verification_scale_interval\": 1} with units implied? Attributes{} is free-form; I could use structured values: {\"maximum_capacity\": {\"value\": 3, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\"}, ...}. That's heavy × 42. Alternatively keep attributes minimal and rely on model_level. The R60 example shows models[] entries with only model_id. I'll include just model_id plus maybe interval_type and a \"models_covered\" for the SW-1 group. Keep it light:\n\n{\"model_id\": \"ED single-interval 3 kg\", \"attributes\": {\"interval_type\": \"single\", \"maximum_capacity_kg\": 3, \"minimum_capacity_g\": 20, \"verification_scale_interval_g\": 1}}\n\nHmm, duplication with model_level. The R137 example does duplicate (matrix_tables + model_level). I'll do models[] with full attributes (simple values, units in key names) AND model_level matrices with structured values. Actually that's 42×4 = 168 lines... fine.\n\nAlternatively skip attributes{} in models[] to reduce bulk, since model_level captures it. The R60 example has no attributes in models. I'll include minimal attributes: {\"interval_type\": \"single\"}. And for SW-1 group: {\"base_model\": \"SW-1\"}. For ER PLUS M group: nothing extra.\n\nLet me go with light models[] entries.\n\nNow build model_level matrices.\n\n**maximum_capacity (kg):**\nSingle:\n- ED single-interval 3 kg: 3\n- ED single-interval 6 kg: 6\n- ED single-interval 15 kg: 15\n- ED single-interval 30 kg: 30\n- ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single-interval 6 kg: 6\n- ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single-interval 15 kg: 15\n- ER PLUS/ER PLUS M single-interval 30 kg: 30\n- SW-1S/SW-1C/SW-1W/SW-1LR/SW-1WR single-interval 2 kg: 2\n- ... 3: 3\n- ... 5: 5\n- ... 6: 6\n- ... 10: 10\n- ... 15: 15\n- ... 20: 20\n- ... 30: 30\n- ER JR single-interval 6: 6\n- ER JR single-interval 15: 15\n- ER JR single-interval 30: 30\n- PW-II single-interval 2: 2\n- PW-II single-interval 5: 5\n- PW-II single-interval 10: 10\nDual (strings):\n- ED dual-interval 1.5/3 kg: \"1.5/3\"\n- ED dual-interval 3/6 kg: \"3/6\"\n- ED dual-interval 6/15 kg: \"6/15\"\n- ED dual-interval 15/30 kg: \"15/30\"\n- ER PLUS/ER PLUS M dual-interval 3/6 kg: \"3/6\"\n- ER PLUS/ER PLUS M dual-interval 6/15 kg: \"6/15\"\n- ER PLUS/ER PLUS M dual-interval 15/30 kg: \"15/30\"\n- SW-1* dual-interval 1/2 kg: \"1/2\"\n- ... 1.5/3: \"1.5/3\"\n- ... 2.5/5: \"2.5/5\"\n- ... 3/6: \"3/6\"\n- ... 4/10: \"4/10\"\n- ... 6/15: \"6/15\"\n- ... 10/20: \"10/20\"\n- ... 15/30: \"15/30\"\n- ER JR dual-interval 3/6: \"3/6\"\n- ER JR dual-interval 6/15: \"6/15\"\n- ER JR dual-interval 15/30: \"15/30\"\n- PW-II dual-interval 1/2: \"1/2\"\n- PW-II dual-interval 2.5/5: \"2.5/5\"\n- PW-II dual-interval 4/10: \"4/10\"\n\n**minimum_capacity (g):**\nSingle:\n- ED: 20, 40, 100, 200\n- ER PLUS: 40, 100, 200\n- SW-1: 20, 20, 40, 40, 100, 100, 200, 200\n- ER JR: 40, 100, 200\n- PW-II: 20, 40, 100\nDual:\n- ED: 10, 20, 40, 100\n- ER PLUS: 20, 40, 100\n- SW-1: 10, 10, 20, 20, 40, 40, 100, 100\n- ER JR: 20, 40, 100\n- PW-II: 10, 20, 40\n\n**verification_scale_interval e (g):**\nSingle:\n- ED: 1, 2, 5, 10\n- ER PLUS: 2, 5, 10\n- SW-1: 1, 1, 2, 2, 5, 5, 10, 10\n- ER JR: 2, 5, 10\n- PW-II: 1, 2, 5\nDual:\n- ED: \"0.5/1\", \"1/2\", \"2/5\", \"5/10\"\n- ER PLUS: \"1/2\", \"2/5\", \"5/10\"\n- SW-1: \"0.5/1\", \"0.5/1\", \"1/2\", \"1/2\", \"2/5\", \"2/5\", \"5/10\", \"5/10\"\n- ER JR: \"1/2\", \"2/5\", \"5/10\"\n- PW-II: \"0.5/1\", \"1/2\", \"2/5\"\n\nmatrix_tables: 6 tables, columns [\"model\", \"maximum_capacity_kg\", \"minimum_capacity_g\", \"verification_scale_interval_e_g\"]. Actually per schema example, columns names like \"qmax\" and column_units array. I'll use columns [\"model\", \"max_kg\", \"min_g\", \"e_g\"] with column_units [null, \"kg\", \"g\", \"g\"]. Hmm — if column names include units, column_units redundant. The R137 example did both (\"qmax(m³/h)\" → \"qmax\" + unit in column_units). I'll follow: columns [\"model\", \"max\", \"min\", \"e\"], column_units [null, \"kg\", \"g\", \"g\"].\n\nRows for table 1 (single ED & ER PLUS):\n- {\"model\": \"ED\", \"max\": 3, \"min\": 20, \"e\": 1}\n- {\"model\": \"ED\", \"max\": 6, \"min\": 40, \"e\": 2}\n- {\"model\": \"ED\", \"max\": 15, \"min\": 100, \"e\": 5}\n- {\"model\": \"ED\", \"max\": 30, \"min\": 200, \"e\": 10}\n- {\"model\": \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\", \"max\": 6, \"min\": 40, \"e\": 2}\n- {\"model\": \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\", \"max\": 15, \"min\": 100, \"e\": 5}\n- {\"model\": \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\", \"max\": 30, \"min\": 200, \"e\": 10}\n\nTable 2 (single SW-1 group):\n- {\"model\": \"SW-1S / SW-1C / SW-1W / SW-1LR / SW-1WR\", \"max\": 2, \"min\": 20, \"e\": 1}\n- {same, 3, 20, 1}\n- {5, 40, 2}\n- {6, 40, 2}\n- {10, 100, 5}\n- {15, 100, 5}\n- {20, 200, 10}\n- {30, 200, 10}\n\nTable 3 (single ER JR, PW-II):\n- {\"model\": \"ER JR\", 6, 40, 2}\n- {15, 100, 5}\n- {30, 200, 10}\n- {\"model\": \"PW-II\", 2, 20, 1}\n- {5, 40, 2}\n- {10, 100, 5}\n\nTable 4 (dual ED & ER PLUS):\n- {\"model\": \"ED\", \"max\": \"1.5 / 3\", \"min\": 10, \"e\": \"0.5 / 1\"}\n- {\"model\": \"ED\", \"max\": \"3 / 6\", \"min\": 20, \"e\": \"1 / 2\"}\n- {\"model\": \"ED\", \"max\": \"6 / 15\", \"min\": 40, \"e\": \"2 / 5\"}\n- {\"model\": \"ED\", \"max\": \"15 / 30\", \"min\": 100, \"e\": \"5 / 10\"}\n- {\"model\": \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\", \"max\": \"3 / 6\", \"min\": 20, \"e\": \"1 / 2\"}\n- {\"model\": \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\", \"max\": \"6 / 15\", \"min\": 40, \"e\": \"2 / 5\"}\n- {\"model\": \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\", \"max\": \"15 / 30\", \"min\": 100, \"e\": \"5 / 10\"}\n\nTable 5 (dual SW-1):\n- 1 / 2, 10, 0.5 / 1\n- 1.5 / 3, 10, 0.5 / 1\n- 2.5 / 5, 20, 1 / 2\n- 3 / 6, 20, 1 / 2\n- 4 / 10, 40, 2 / 5\n- 6 / 15, 40, 2 / 5\n- 10 / 20, 100, 5 / 10\n- 15 / 30, 100, 5 / 10\n\nTable 6 (dual ER JR, PW-II):\n- ER JR: 3 / 6, 20, 1 / 2\n- ER JR: 6 / 15, 40, 2 / 5\n- ER JR: 15 / 30, 100, 5 / 10\n- PW-II: 1 / 2, 10, 0.5 / 1\n- PW-II: 2.5 / 5, 20, 1 / 2\n- PW-II: 4 / 10, 40, 2 / 5\n\nDescription for matrix tables: \"Single-interval instruments: capacity variants of ED and ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\" etc.\n\nNow decision: 42 models in model_family.models — I'll include attributes with interval_type only to keep concise? Or full? I'll include attributes: {\"interval_type\": \"single\"} etc. plus \"capacity_variant\"? The model_id already encodes capacity. Just interval_type.\n\nHmm, actually, maybe attributes could hold the three values in structured form for consumer convenience. But it doubles the data. The spec example R137 doesn't include attributes in models. I'll keep just interval_type.\n\nNow — one more consideration: should \"ER PLUS M\" be \"ER PLUS-M\"? Table header: \"ER PLUS / ER PLUS M\". Description: \"ER PLUS-M\". I'll use \"ER PLUS M\" in model ids (matching table) and include both spellings in type_designations.\n\nAlso description of the group in model_level values' model field must match model_id exactly for joinability. OK.\n\nPower supply voltage — should I make this model_level with grouped model labels that don't match model_ids? Rule doesn't forbid; but consistency... I could instead put it in config_level with axis \"model group\":\n- attribute: \"power_supply_voltage\", axis: \"model\", values: [{condition: \"ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1 series, ER JR\", value: 12, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"PW-II\", value: 9, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]\nThat fits config_level semantics better (condition-based). And battery options similarly with axis \"model\".\n\nI'll use config_level for: power_supply_voltage (axis \"model group\"), battery_option (axis \"model group\"), subtractive_tare_range (axis \"interval type\").\n\nWait but rule 4 says attributes varying by model go in model_level. Power supply voltage varies by model → model_level. But the model axis values are groups... I could split: 12V applies to all ED/ER PLUS/ER PLUS M/SW-1/ER JR variants (33 variants... let me count: single: ED 4 + ERM 3 + SW1 8 + ERJR 3 = 18; dual: ED 4 + ERM 3 + SW1 8 + ERJR 3 = 18; total 36 variants at 12 V; PW-II: 6 variants at 9 V). Enumerating 42 entries for voltage is excessive. I'll use config_level with model-group conditions — pragmatic and informative.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. {attribute: \"power_supply_voltage\", axis: \"model group\", values: [{condition: \"ED, ER PLUS, ER PLUS M, SW-1S, SW-1C, SW-1W, SW-1LR, SW-1WR, ER JR\", value: 12, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"PW-II\", value: 9, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]}\n2. {attribute: \"power_supply_type\", ...} — type_level instead: {value: \"DC\"}. Actually mains adaptor supplies 12/9 V DC; instrument may also run on battery. type_level: power_supply_type \"DC\" and \"battery\". Hmm, value as string \"DC\". Keep: type_level \"power_supply\": {value: \"mains or battery\"}. Eh. I'll do:\n   - type_level: {\"mains_or_battery_operated\": {value: true}}? Simpler: description covers it. I'll add type_level power_supply_type: {value: \"DC\"}.\n3. {attribute: \"battery_option\", axis: \"model group\", values: [\n     {condition: \"ED, ER PLUS, ER JR\", value: \"integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.6 Ah\"},\n     {condition: \"ER JR (alternative)\", value: \"integrated 4 x 1.5 V D type batteries\"},\n     {condition: \"SW-1\", value: \"integrated rechargeable 6 V 3.5 Ah\"},\n     {condition: \"PW-II\", value: \"integrated 6 x 1.5 V AA batteries\"}\n   ]}\n   Note ER JR has two options. Values as strings.\n\ntype_level characteristics:\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"III\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- temperature_range: {value: {min: -10, max: 40}, unit_symbol: \"°C\", unit_id: \"u:degree_Celsius\"}\n- initial_zero_setting_range: {value: {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol: \"%\"} — of Max. Rename \"initial_zero_setting_range_of_max\"? I'll use the explicit names to preserve \"of Max\":\n  - \"initial_zero_setting_range_of_max\"\n  - \"semi_automatic_zero_setting_range_of_max\"\n  - \"zero_tracking_range_of_max\"\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"DC\"}\n\nHmm footnote_markers: include empty array or omit? Schema shows footnote_markers in shape; I'll include only when present. Rule 2 shape includes footnote_markers — example shows it. I'll include \"footnote_markers\": [] where relevant? To keep clean, omit empty. The strict shape says every characteristic value MUST be object with that shape including footnote_markers. I'll include \"footnote_markers\": [] to be safe.\n\nLet me include footnote_markers: [] in all structured values then. That adds bulk × ~140 values. Hmm. The rule: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\" — shape includes footnote_markers. I'll include it everywhere, even empty. It's verbose but compliant. Actually for matrix_tables rows, plain numbers are used in the example (no structured shape) — rows are free-form objects per example. OK.\n\nFor model_level values, shape: {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\" at attribute level? In the R137 example, model_level entries are {\"attribute\", \"unit_symbol\", \"values\":[{\"model\",\"value\"}]} — so unit at attribute level, values just model+value. Footnote markers would be on individual values if needed. I'll follow that example exactly: attribute-level unit_symbol, per-value {model, value}. No footnote_markers per value (none needed). \n\nFor type_level: full structured shape with unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers.\n\nComponents:\n- {\"role\": \"load cell\", \"type_designations\": [\"SW\"], \"alternatives\": null, \"characteristics\": {\"manufacturer\": \"CAS\"}}\nShould I include the load cell also as... fine.\n\nWait — is the \"Load cell: The load cell is a CAS load cell, model SW.\" a component of the weighing instrument. Yes.\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: \"R76\"\n- edition: 1992\n- amendment: null — the \"(E)\" denotes English; skip or null. I'll omit amendment (rule 8: omit if not present). Or set null. I'll set null.\n- scheme: omit/null — MAA. I'll omit.\n- accuracy_classes: [\"III\"]\n\nTest reports:\n- [{\"id\": \"P00945\", \"date\": null, \"pages\": 24, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"}] — omit date since not present. Include \"description\"? Not in schema. Fine.\n\nRevision history: as above with revision strings \"0\",\"1\",\"2\",\"3\". The first issue: \"R76/1992-GB1-10.04\" on 27 May 2009 = revision 0 effectively; the table lists it without \"rev\". I'll use revision \"\" → hmm revision should be string like \"0\". The table's first row has no rev number. Options: revision: \"0\" (inferred) or revision: \"original\". I'll use \"0\" — that's a reasonable canonicalization? It says revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). Using \"0\" for first issue is an inference but standard. I'll do it.\n\ndates: \"2009-05-27\", \"2012-09-04\", \"2014-05-12\", \"2014-05-19\".\n\nChanges text:\n- rev1: \"Load cell and technical data sections added.\"\n- rev2: \"Single-interval instruments added. Max tare value added to devices section. Sealing section added.\"\n- rev3: \"Applicant's address changed from: 19 Ganap-Ri, Gwangjuk-Myoun, Yangju-Si, Gyeonggi-Do 482-841, Republic of Korea. Alternative manufacturer added.\"\n\nFootnotes: none → omit.\n\ndate_issued: \"2014-05-19\".\n\nNow, also \"Signatory: G Stones\" — no schema field; skip.\n\nUKAS \"0135\" — could be issuer id? \"UKAS PRODUCT CERTIFICATION 0135\" is a UKAS accredited certification body number, not OIML issuer id. Omit oiml_issuer_id.\n\ncertificate.scheme: The schema expects \"A\" or \"B\". This is an MAA certificate — not part of OIML-CS A/B. Omit.\n\nLet me also double check member_state: \"United Kin